    {
      "title": "Visa, Mastercard, JPMorgan customer data exposed in ransomware attack",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/visa-mastercard-jpmorgan-customer-data-leak-ransomware-attack-us-outage-126112000197_1.html",
      "description": "A ransomware group has leaked customer data linked to Visa, Mastercard and JPMorgan Chase after targeting third-party service providers, exposing thousands of email addresses and some phone numbers",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-20T03:54:41"
    },
    {
      "title": "Cyberattack leaks JPMorgan Chase customer emails, some phone numbers",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/technology/tech-news/cyberattack-leaks-thousands-of-chase-customer-emails-some-phone-numbers-126112000035_1.html",
      "description": "Personal information belonging to thousands of JPMorgan Chase customers was leaked onto the internet after third-party vendors at the bank were targeted in a cyberattack. The leak is unrelated to a bank outage earlier this week and the information involved did not include credit card or particularly sensitive personal information, the bank said.\nThe leaked data appears to be mostly email addresses of Chase customers as well as some bank employees, as well as their phone numbers in some cases.\n\"We have learned that third parties who provide services to the firm were the target of a cyber attack,\" said Joe Evangelisti, a spokesperson for the bank on Thursday.\nBased on our investigation so far, we believe the attackers accessed certain marketing data held by those vendors, which may include some customer names and email addresses.\nVisa and Mastercard also confirmed the data breach, but the hack had no impact on company functions or customer payment information.\nThe accounts impacted,",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-20T02:45:05"
    }
  ]
